							<h1>Medicare Enrollment Seasonal Forecasting</h1>
							<span class="image main"><img src="images/05_forecast.png" alt="Total Medicare enrollment, actual and 12 month forecast" /></span>

						<main>
        <section>
            <p>Medicare open enrollment runs October 15 to December 7 each year, with coverage taking effect January 1. I went into this project assuming that window drives a seasonal pattern in enrollment, and that a seasonal index built from history could be used to project forward for staffing and capacity planning.</p>

            <p>That assumption turned out to be wrong. This analysis is built around finding out why, and what a planner should do instead.</p>

            <p>Data is pulled live from the CMS data API rather than a downloaded file, so re-running the notebook always uses the newest data CMS has published. The series covers 159 monthly observations, January 2013 through March 2026, with no gaps.</p>

            <ul class="actions">
                <li><a href="https://github.com/maya-maison/medicare-enrollment-forecasting" class="button primary" target="_blank">View the notebook on GitHub</a></li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Problem and Approach</h2>
            <p>The goal was a defensible twelve month forecast of Medicare enrollment for capacity and financial planning. My process:</p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <h3>Decomposition:</h3>
                    <p>I estimated the trend with a 2x12 centered moving average and detrended the series both additively and multiplicatively. I wrote down my expectation before running the test, predicting a multiplicative model because open enrollment is a rate rather than a fixed quota. The test proved me wrong. The correlation between the size of the seasonal swing and the level of the series was 0.078, effectively zero, and the year to year noise was larger than any systematic growth. I chose multiplicative as a documented tie break and said plainly that the test was inconclusive.</p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <h3>Seasonal Profile:</h3>
                    <p>Total enrollment swings only 0.176 percent peak to trough, roughly 124,000 beneficiaries against a base of 70 million. November and December, the two months at the center of the open enrollment window, carry the weakest and noisiest signal of any month in the series.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <figure>
                <img src="images/01_raw_series.png" alt="National Medicare enrollment, January 2013 to March 2026" style="width: 100%; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
                <figcaption>Total enrollment grows steadily from 51.7M to 70.2M. At this scale, no seasonality is visible at all.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <br /><br />

            <ol start="3">
                <li>
                    <h3>Testing the Mechanism:</h3>
                    <p>Open enrollment does not add people to Medicare. Eligibility is reached by turning 65, which happens continuously in every month of the year. What the enrollment window governs is which <em>plan</em> a beneficiary holds. If that is true, the seasonal effect is real but invisible in the total, because switching moves people between categories that sum to the same number.</p>
                    <p>I split the total into Original Medicare and Medicare Advantage and confirmed the two parts sum exactly, in all 159 months. The component profiles are near perfect mirror images, correlating at <strong>-0.99</strong>. The seasonal effect in the components is roughly <strong>19 times larger</strong> than in the total.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <figure>
                <img src="images/03_january_switch.png" alt="Average month over month change in Medicare enrollment by month" style="width: 100%; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
                <figcaption>Roughly 794,000 beneficiaries switch from Original Medicare to Medicare Advantage every January. The two components cancel almost exactly, which is why total enrollment barely moves.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <br /><br />
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Key Findings</h2>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <h3>The seasonality lives in composition, not volume:</h3>
                    <p>Open enrollment does not change how many people have Medicare. It changes which plan they hold. The effect nets to zero in the total and only appears when the total is split into its parts. A planner looking at total enrollment would conclude there is no seasonality worth modeling, and would be looking at the wrong series.</p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <h3>The switching regime has ended:</h3>
                    <p>Before projecting the seasonal index forward, I tested whether the pattern behind it was still stable. It was not.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <section>
                <h2>January Plan Switching by Year</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Year</th>
                            <th>January Switches</th>
                            <th>% of Advantage Base</th>
                            <th>Advantage Share</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>2021</td><td>1,557,000</td><td>6.11%</td><td>42.6%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2022</td><td>1,362,000</td><td>4.87%</td><td>45.5%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2023</td><td>1,268,000</td><td>4.19%</td><td>47.9%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2024</td><td>1,007,000</td><td>3.09%</td><td>49.9%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2025</td><td>450,000</td><td>1.31%</td><td>50.6%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2026</td><td>99,000</td><td>0.28%</td><td>50.9%</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <style>
