            <p>Five consecutive years of decline, down <strong>94 percent</strong> from the 2021 peak. Medicare Advantage crossed half the market and effectively stopped growing. The historical January index of 1.016 describes a boom that is over. Applied to January 2026, it would have predicted roughly 885,000 switches against an actual of 99,000, an overstatement of <strong>nine times</strong>.</p>

            <ol start="3">
                <li>
                    <h3>A correlation of 0.047 that means the opposite of what it looks like:</h3>
                    <p>The correlation between January switch volume and year is 0.047. Read alone, that says no relationship. It is a trap. Correlation only measures a straight line, and this relationship rises to a 2021 peak and then collapses. Split at the peak, the rising phase correlates at +0.89 and the falling phase at <strong>-0.97</strong>, both significant. A correlation near zero does not mean no relationship. It means no <em>linear</em> relationship.</p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <h3>An R-squared of 0.9984 that hides a structural break:</h3>
                    <p>A linear trend fit to the full series returns an R-squared of 0.9984 and is still the wrong model. The residuals arc rather than scatter, revealing a COVID era enrollment deficit of roughly 1.17 million beneficiaries. The growth rate recovered by 2024. The level never did.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <figure>
                <img src="images/04_trend_residuals.png" alt="Residuals from the linear trend" style="width: 100%; max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
                <figcaption>The residuals are patterned, not random. Random scatter would mean the line fits. An arc means it is missing something. R-squared does not detect curvature.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <br /><br />

            <p>Because of that break, the fitted line sits roughly 300,000 beneficiaries above the last actual observation. A forecast starting from that line would begin 300,000 too high and stay there. I refit the trend on the post recovery window and anchored the projection to the last actual data point rather than to the line, then documented what that choice was worth: roughly 233,000 beneficiaries over twelve months.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What a Planner Should Do With This</h2>

            <p>Total enrollment can be forecast, and the forecast is simple. Roughly 71.6 million by March 2027, growing about 1.43 million over the year. No seasonal adjustment is needed, because the largest seasonal effect in any month is about 65,000 people out of 70 million, smaller than normal month to month variation.</p>

            <p><strong>But the workload is not in the total. It is in the switching.</strong> Processing a plan change, updating the record, taking the call, none of that scales with how many people have Medicare. It scales with how many people are moving. And the switching has collapsed.</p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>An operation that staffs January 2027 off the ten year average would prepare for roughly 885,000 switches. The most recent actual was 99,000.</strong> That is nine times the work that is actually coming.</li>
                <li><strong>This runs opposite to intuition.</strong> Medicare operations have spent a decade building January surge capacity. The surge is the thing that is going away.</li>
                <li><strong>The seasonal index would not have caught this.</strong> Built on thirteen years, January comes back at 1.016 and looks completely stable. The collapse only shows up when the index is tested year by year rather than trusted as an average.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>What this data cannot say</h3>
            <p>Saturation at roughly 51 percent is the obvious explanation and it fits the timing. Medicare Advantage market disruptions over the same period, including plan exits and payment changes, would produce a similar signature. This analysis shows what happened. It cannot separate the causes, and the recommendation depends on which is true: if the cause is saturation, January capacity should be permanently reduced. If plans exited markets, switching could return and capacity should stay flexible. Telling those apart requires plan availability by county, benchmark rates, and plan level enrollment, which is the next analysis.</p>

            <h3>Recommendation</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Forecast total enrollment on trend. It is reliable.</li>
                <li>Do not forecast January switching from this history. The pattern it is built on is gone.</li>
                <li>Size January 2027 capacity to recent actuals, not the ten year average.</li>
                <li>Watch the January 2027 number. It is the test of whether the decline has bottomed out.</li>
            </ul>

            <p><em>A forecast that cannot be defended is worse than no forecast, because someone will staff to it.</em></p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Methods</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Ratio to moving average decomposition, using a 2x12 centered moving average</li>
                <li>Additive versus multiplicative model testing, with the prior stated before the test</li>
                <li>Seasonal index construction and normalization</li>
                <li>Seasonal index stability testing across the full history</li>
                <li>Linear trend fitting with residual pattern diagnostics</li>
                <li>Trend projection anchored at the forecast origin</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Tools</h3>
            <p>Python &middot; Pandas &middot; NumPy &middot; Matplotlib &middot; Jupyter &middot; CMS data API</p>
        </section>

